Body:
I think `kotlin‑lsp` works with opencode.ai on Unix‑like systems, including WSL.  
I use it on both Linux and macOS with the following setup:

1. Follow the installation instructions at <https://github.com/Kotlin/kotlin-lsp>.  
2. Add the following configuration to your `opencode.json`:

```diff
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
  "provider": {
    "llama-cpp": {
      "npm": "@ai-sdk/openai-compatible",
      "name": "Llama.cpp (local)",
      "options": {
        "baseURL": "http://localhost:11434/v1"
      },
      "models": {
        "gpt-oss-20B": {
          "name": "unsloth/gpt-oss-20b-GGUF:Q4_K_M"
        }
      }
    }
  },
  "lsp": {
+    "kotlin-lsp": {
+     "command": ["kotlin-lsp"],
+      "extensions": [".kt"]
+    }
  }
}
```

@GeorgeFlorian I would highly recommend adding this to your global opencode.json (this is taken directly from my own):
```
{
  "permission": {
      "bash": {
        "rm *": "ask",
        "git push *": "ask",
        "git commit *": "ask",
      },
    },
}
```

Even with instructions in an AGENTS.md or equivalent, an llm isn't gonna always listen. For these more critical things I like deterministic solutions like permissions. 

But oddly I've also never had it try to commit anything for me, maybe just introducing the idea makes it more likely to happen

I was using Claude Sonnet 4.5 from GitHub Copilot.

Here is my `opencode.json` config for reference:
```
    "slack": {
      "type": "local",
      "command": ["npx", "-y", "slack-mcp-server@latest", "--transport", "stdio"],
      "environment": {
        "SLACK_MCP_XOXC_TOKEN": "xoxc-xxx",
        "SLACK_MCP_XOXD_TOKEN": "xoxd-xxx"
      }
    }
```

Claude Desktop configuration (`claude_desktop_config`):
```
    "slack": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": [
        "-y",
        "slack-mcp-server@latest",
        "--transport",
        "stdio"
      ],
      "env": {
        "SLACK_MCP_XOXC_TOKEN": "xoxc-xxx",
        "SLACK_MCP_XOXD_TOKEN": "xoxd-xxx"
      }
    }
```
